---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getUrl } from '../../utils/nav';
---

<BaseLayout title="Commercial Journey Transformation" description="Cross-functional service-design case study">
  <main class="page-shell project-detail-shell">
    <article class="project-detail">
      <div class="project-header">
        <a href={getUrl('/projects')} class="back-link"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> All case studies</a>
        <h1>Commercial Journey Transformation</h1>
        <p class="project-intro">Mapped a complex commercial journey to surface the operational friction keeping customer-facing teams from spending time with customers.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="project-meta-grid">
        <div class="meta-item">
          <span class="meta-label">Client:</span>
          <span class="meta-value">Global B2B technology company</span>
        </div>
        <div class="meta-item">
          <span class="meta-label">Role:</span>
          <span class="meta-value">Service designer & researcher</span>
        </div>
        <div class="meta-item">
          <span class="meta-label">Focus:</span>
          <span class="meta-value">Journey mapping, workshop design, operational alignment</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="project-content">
        <section class="content-section">
          <h2>Giving a transformation effort a shared view of the work</h2>
          <p>A commercial organization knew that its customer-facing teams were losing time to administrative tasks, unclear handoffs, and inconsistent processes. It did not have a shared view of the complete experience, from first customer conversation through onboarding, billing, and ongoing support.</p>
          <p>Service Design was asked to help make the work visible: understand where friction accumulated, align the people responsible for different parts of the journey, and identify practical opportunities for improvement under a short timeline.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-section">
          <h2>My contribution</h2>
          <p>I worked across discovery, workshop design, facilitation, synthesis, and final deliverables. I conducted pre-work stakeholder interviews; designed the templates and facilitation materials; co-facilitated small-group sessions; captured and organized evidence during the working sessions; and helped turn the output into a clear, usable journey map.</p>
          <p>The effort brought together more than 30 participants from customer-facing, operational, and technical functions. My job was to help a diverse group describe a complex process in a common language and move from individual frustrations toward shared priorities.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-section">
          <h2>From fragmented views to a single journey</h2>
          <p>Before the workshop, we interviewed stakeholders across the commercial lifecycle to understand tensions before asking a large group to map them together. A consistent pattern emerged: information, ownership, and decision-making often broke down at the boundaries between teams.</p>
          <p>The resulting journey connected five broad phases: customer acquisition, sales engagement, implementation, billing, and ongoing use and support. Rather than treating problems as isolated incidents, we placed recurring friction at the moments where it occurred and used that evidence to guide the discussion.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-section">
          <h2>What we learned</h2>
          <p>Three themes recurred across the journey. First, early administrative steps created delays before customer-facing teams could make progress. Second, fragmented tools and inconsistent decision rules made it difficult to produce timely, reliable commercial proposals. Finally, information captured before a sale did not always travel cleanly into delivery, leaving customers and teams to repeat work after the handoff.</p>
          <p>Framing these issues as structural process and role-design challenges, rather than failures of individual teams, helped the group identify improvements they could own together.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-section">
          <h2>Outcome</h2>
          <p>The work produced a validated, end-to-end journey map and a prioritized set of improvement opportunities, each anchored to a specific point in the experience. The artifact gave the transformation team a traceable evidence base for discussing where to simplify work, clarify ownership, and improve continuity for customers.</p>
          <p>Over time, the organization introduced changes that aligned with the journey's recommendations, including simplifying commercial workflows and strengthening the bridge between sales and delivery. While this project was one input among many, it established a shared baseline for the work and a practical way to keep the conversation grounded in the people doing it.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-section">
          <h2>What I learned</h2>
          <p>This was a formative example of Service Design as business design. I learned how to build enough structure for a large, cross-functional group to synthesize together, capture notes for analysis rather than archival purposes, and create deliverables that can support consequential conversations without oversimplifying the complexity underneath them.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
